"# Chapter 3 — There is no export statement (but public vs private is real)

Chapter 2 said Motoi has no `import`. Its mirror image: Motoi has no `export` either. In a module-having Scheme, `export` names which of a module's verbs the world may use and which stay private. Typing `(export greet farewell)` in Motoi yields `error: unbound symbol: export` — it is another dialect's word.

But the *idea* behind export — a public surface versus a private one — is real and useful, and Motoi expresses it with tools it does have: top-level `define` for the public surface, and lexical scope (`let`, internal `define`, closures) for the private interior. This chapter teaches that idea in Motoi's own terms, and marks the `export` form as a dialect boundary along the way.

## 3.1 The dialect boundary, stated

In some Schemes you write a module wrapper with an export list:

`(module manners (export greet farewell) (define (greet name) ...) ...)`

Motoi has neither `module` nor `export`. You write your `define`s at the top level and they are simply available:

```scheme
(define (greet name)
  (string-append \"Hello, \" name \".\"))

(define (farewell name)
  (string-append \"Goodbye, \" name \".\"))

(list (greet \"Alfred\") (farewell \"Alfred\"))
;; ⇒ (\"Hello, Alfred.\" \"Goodbye, Alfred.\")
```

No wrapper, no export list. The verbs exist because you defined them.

## 3.2 Public vs private, or why hiding is a kindness

The distinction the `export` list *encoded* — public verbs versus internal helpers — still matters enormously. In Motoi you draw the line with scope instead of a list. A helper you nest inside another definition, or hide in a `let`, is private: nothing outside can reach it.

```scheme
(define (flip-run count)
  ;; heads-or-tails is PRIVATE — defined inside flip-run, invisible outside
  (define (heads-or-tails n)
    (if (< n 0.5) 'heads 'tails))
  (let loop ((n count) (heads 0) (tails 0))
    (if (= n 0)
        (list :heads heads :tails tails)
        (case (heads-or-tails (random))
          ((heads) (loop (- n 1) (+ heads 1) tails))
          ((tails) (loop (- n 1) heads (+ tails 1)))))))

(length (flip-run 50))
;; ⇒ 4
```

`heads-or-tails` is a private helper: it lives inside `flip-run` and no other code can call it. `flip-run` is your public surface. This is the same public/private kindness the `export` list gave you — refactor the interior freely, because nothing outside depends on it — achieved with plain scope.

The rule still holds: **keep the smallest public surface that makes your code useful.** If in doubt, nest it. It is easier to promote a helper to the top level later than to claw one back once callers depend on it.

## 3.3 Values and constants are just top-level defines

In the other dialect you would list constants in an `export`. In Motoi, a top-level `define` of a value *is* the public surface — there is nothing extra to declare.

```scheme
(define red   '(1.0 0.0 0.0))
(define green '(0.0 1.0 0.0))
(define blue  '(0.0 0.0 1.0))

(define (color? c)
  (and (list? c)
       (= (length c) 3)
       (every number? c)))

(list red (color? red))
;; ⇒ ((1.0 0.0 0.0) #t)
```

`red`, `green`, `blue` are values; `color?` is a verb; all four are public simply by being top-level. `every` came from SRFI-1, already loaded.

## 3.4 A structure and its operations

Say you build a little structure — a point — out of a tagged list, with a constructor, a predicate, and accessors. In Motoi they are all public the moment you `define` them; no export list gates them.

```scheme
(define (make-point x y) (list 'point x y))
(define (point? p) (and (pair? p) (eq? (car p) 'point)))
(define (point-x p) (cadr p))
(define (point-y p) (caddr p))

(define (square n) (* n n))

(define (point-distance a b)
  (sqrt (+ (square (- (point-x a) (point-x b)))
           (square (- (point-y a) (point-y b))))))

(point-distance (make-point 0 0) (make-point 3 4))
;; ⇒ 5
```

`make-point`, `point?`, `point-x`, `point-y`, `point-distance` are your public surface. If you wanted `square` private, you would nest it inside `point-distance` as in section 3.2. Here it is fine at the top level because it is harmless. (R7RS-small's `define-record-type` gives a sturdier way to build structures; the tagged list here keeps the example about *public versus private*, not about records.)

## 3.5 There is no re-export, because there is nothing to re-export

The other dialect's *facade* pattern re-exports names from several modules under one umbrella module. Motoi has no modules and no export, so there is no facade form. The Motoi equivalent is simpler: the standard library verbs are already loaded everywhere, so you never need an umbrella to gather them. If you want a shorthand name for something, you `define` an alias:

```scheme
(define hello string-append)
(hello \"Hi, \" \"Alfred\")
;; ⇒ \"Hi, Alfred\"
```

That is the whole of it — no umbrella, no re-export, just a `define` when a friendlier name helps.

## 3.6 Renaming — again, just `define`

The other dialect's `export` rename (\"internally `greet-in-english`, publicly `hello`\") maps onto a plain `define` in Motoi:

```scheme
(define (greet-in-english name)
  (string-append \"Hello, \" name \".\"))

(define hello greet-in-english)   ;; the public name

(hello \"Alfred\")
;; ⇒ \"Hello, Alfred.\"
```

The verbose internal name is what you read while working; the short alias is the friendly surface. No export form required.

## 3.7 What to keep private

The old advice about *what not to export* becomes advice about *what to keep out of the top level*:

**Helpers.** If a verb only makes sense next to one other verb, nest it (section 3.2). Its ugly name and internal shape stay hidden.

**Mutable state.** Do not leave a bare mutable top-level variable that any code can read and write. Wrap it in a closure and hand out reader/writer verbs, so access is controlled:

```scheme
(define counter
  (let ((n 0))
    (lambda () (set! n (+ n 1)) n)))

(list (counter) (counter) (counter))
;; ⇒ (1 2 3)
```

The variable `n` is sealed inside the closure — private state, reachable only through `counter`.

**Everything-at-once.** Do not reflexively give every scrap a public top-level name. A small surface is easier to keep stable.

## 3.8 There is no `module-verbs` to introspect

The other dialect lets you ask a module for its export list with `module-verbs`, or enumerate modules with `module-list`. Motoi has neither: `(module-verbs 'anything)` yields `error: unbound symbol: module-verbs`, and there is no `module-list`. There are no module objects to enumerate.

How do you learn what is available in Motoi, then? You read the reference manual, and you call verbs at the REPL — if a name is unbound, Motoi says so. Discovery is by documentation and by trying, not by querying a module record.

## 3.9 There is no `:deprecated` marker

The other dialect's `(export :deprecated tangent)` marks a verb as still-present-but-going-away. Motoi's standard library is fixed and small, so it has no deprecation marker in an export list (there is no export list). If your *own* code needs to warn callers off an old verb, you write the warning by hand inside it — the language does not provide an `export`-level marker, because it does not provide `export`.

## 3.10 The real contract in Motoi

Without `import`/`export`, what is the contract between code and its callers? It is plain scope and plain naming:

- **What your code offers** — its top-level `define`s.
- **What it hides** — everything nested in a `let`, closure, or inner `define`.
- **What callers may assume** — a top-level name means what its definition says; a private helper is not theirs to reach.

Motoi enforces the private half strictly: lexical scope is not advisory, it is airtight. A name inside a closure genuinely cannot be reached from outside. That is a stronger guarantee than an honor-system export list, and it is built into the language rather than layered on top.

## 3.11 Try it

Write two top-level verbs that share one private helper — nest the helper inside a `let` that wraps both, or inside one of them. Confirm the helper is unreachable from the top level (calling it yields `unbound symbol`). Then wrap a counter in a closure as in section 3.7 and watch its state stay sealed. That is public-versus-private in Motoi, with no `export` in sight.

Chapter 4 covers *discovery* in the other dialect — where a module system looks for a module — and, for Motoi, why there is nothing to look for. Turn the page.")
